course: dictionary
lesson: 1482
title: A word that only comes in a group
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Most nouns have a singular and a plural: one cow, two cows. Cattle is different. It has no singular form, so you never say one cattle. To talk about one animal, say «a cow», «a bull» or «a head of cattle».
explain: Cattle means the whole group of large farm animals, such as cows, bulls and steers. Look at the words around it to check: «Cattle, sheep, goats and pigs were tamed in South-West Asia» puts cattle in a list of farm animals.
explain: In maths problems, cattle are counted as heads, so «200 head of cattle» means 200 animals. The word «herd» is a group of animals living or moving together, so «herd of cattle» means a group of cows and bulls.
fact: Cattle is a plural noun with no singular form.
fact: To mean one animal, use «a cow», «a bull» or «a head of cattle».
fact: «Herd of cattle» means a group of cattle together.
fact: «Dogs were the first tamed animals, long before farming; sheep, goats, cattle and pigs came later, and horses much later.»
fact: In maths, cattle are counted as heads: «200 head of cattle» means 200 animals.
exercise: Cattle are large farm animals. What is cattle? | One single cow | A kind of grass | *A group of farm animals such as cows, bulls and steers | A bag of seeds for the farm
exercise: A farmer counts his «200 head of cattle». What does this mean? | 200 cattle sheds | 200 bales of hay | 200 cows only | *200 animals in the herd
exercise: Fill the gap: «The cattle ___ grazing in the field.» | *are | is | cattles | was
test: Which phrase is the right way to talk about one animal from the herd? | cattle one | cattles | *a cow | many cattle
test: What does «dairy cattle» mean? | Cattle that live in the sea | Wild cattle that live in the hills | Cattle kept only for their leather, never for milk | *Cattle kept for milk
exam: Which sentence uses cattle correctly? | One cattle is eating grass. | A cattles are eating grass. | The cattle is eating grass. | *The cattle are eating grass.
